                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{fullness}:\sum_{F:U \times U \to U} \prod_{A:U} \prod_{B:U} T(F(A, B)) \simeq \sum_{R:T(A) \times T(B) \to U} \prod_{x:T(A)} \left(\prod_{y:T(A)} \mathrm{isProp}(T(R(x, y)))\right) \times \exists y:T(A).T(R(x, y))
          %p
            If the dependent type theory does not have any type universes at all, one could still express the axiom of fullness by explicitly postulating the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+entire+relations"} type of all entire relations
            using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
          %h2#relation_to_other_axioms Relation to other axioms
          %p
            In the presence of the usual basic axioms of set theory (in particular, the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+separation"} bounded separation
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+pairing"} pairing
            in a material set theory and the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalisers"} equalisers
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            in a structural set theory), we have:
          %blockquote
            %p power sets → fullness → function sets
          %p
            On the one hand,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{X,Y}
            can be constructed as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(X \times Y)
            consisting of
            %em all
            entire relations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            may be constructed as a subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{X,Y}
            consisting of those elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq R
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            (the graph of) some function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = R
            must follow when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is entire and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a function). Conversely, if we have function sets, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            states precisely that the function set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            always qualifies as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{X,Y}
            (although function sets and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            are already sufficient for power sets and thus fullness).
          %p
            Fullness also follows from function sets in the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+omniscience"} principle of omniscience
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+set"} projective set
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon P \to X
            (which this axiom asserts must exist), then we may take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^P
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{X,Y}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,a,b) \in E_{X,Y}
            iff, for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(c) = a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(c) = b
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{X,Y}
            is not categorical (in the logicians' sense); that is, there may be many sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, F', \ldots
            that all satisfy the axiom without being (in an appropriate sense) equal. This is the downside to adopting the fullness axiom, compared to power sets or function sets; this is also why the internal version is complicated.
          %p
            As fullness is weaker than power sets in a constructive framework, we may consider it
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            in the sense of the constructive school; this is one reason for adopting it while rejecting power sets.
          %h2#definition_of_the_real_numbers Definition of the real numbers
          %p
            One reason for adopting the axiom of fullness instead of (only) function sets is this: it is strong enough to construct the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            of (located Dedekind)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            from the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %em without
            assuming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+reals"} Cauchy reals
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            of the function set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            principle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} AC
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{AC}_{\mathbb{N},2}
            that follows from either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            (classically accepted) or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            (as a statement in the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell–Bénabou language
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers+object"} real numbers object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Of course, in a topos, there are other ways to define
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %em also
            be predicative? If you adopt the fullness axiom, then you can modify the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            to produce a definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %em without
            assuming any choice principle. Instead of using
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\mathbb{N},\mathbb{N}}
            instead;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            can be constructed as a subquotient of that set.
          %p
            Let an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\mathbb{N},\mathbb{N}}
            be a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivalued+function"} multivalued
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of natural numbers, and fix a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Q}
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            %em regular
            if, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j,x,y
            are natural numbers such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(i,x)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(j,y)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi j
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\phi(x) - \phi(y)|} \leq 1/i + 1/j .
          %p
            (It is common, but not really necessary in the presence of function sets, to use regular sequences instead of the more general Cauchy sequences when defining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            in constructive mathematics, and we follow that here.) We call two regular multivalued sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r,s
            %em equivalent
            if, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j,x,y
            are natural numbers such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(i,x)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(j,y)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi j
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\phi(x) - \phi(y)|} \leq 1/i + 1/j .
          %p
            Then this defines an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            on the set of regular multivalued sequences, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+complete"} Dedekind complete
            relative to located pairs of upper and lower sets.)
          %p
            However, on one hand, if one’s goal is to have the real numbers, the axiom of fullness is overkill: one can simply just postulate the real numbers in any number of ways, such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers+object"} real numbers object
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+complete"} Dedekind complete
            relative to located pairs of upper and lower sets. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resizing+axiom"} resizing axiom
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cuts"} Dedekind cuts
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
